6. Bh4
{ The main move. Bishop retreats to h4, retaining the bishop pair and maintaining chances to build up pressure later. White can be more ambitious by leaving minor pieces on the board. }
( 6. Bxf6
{ This is the anti-Tarrasch. The idea is to remove a defender of d5 so that after black takes back, white can play e3, open a line for the bishop, then rook to c1. Black gets a semi-Slav type structure where the light square bishop is trapped behind the pawns. }
